+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server assigns each committed edit a monotone revision number. Browsers need to see those committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, recover a current board after up to 20 minutes of disconnection, and handle opening-time bursts of up to 20 edits per second across 800 simultaneous browsers.
+
+The delivery mechanism is the architectural load-bearer for these requirements. The choice determines what the change store must maintain, how delivery manages connections, and what browsers must do to stay current or recover. Three classes of mechanism exist in this problem space.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Criteria
+
+- **Change freshness under load** (major) — how quickly a committed edit reaches connected browsers during opening-time bursts. At 20 edits per second, systematic lag compounds; browsers that are persistently behind the board mislead coordinators. The 15-second goal is a ceiling, not a target.
+- **Read load under busy conditions** (moderate) — request traffic the server sustains in steady state, independent of the edit rate. Unnecessary baseline traffic competes with write throughput during bursts.
+- **Operational independence** (major) — the degree to which board availability and cost depend on a third-party provider. Turns on the tenet *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted*: the latitude exists, but the decision must weigh what coupling and ongoing fees mean for a pilot-scale deployment.
+- **Connection management complexity** (moderate) — server and browser work introduced by the mechanism: session tracking, reconnect handling, catch-up logic. This work is not avoided but allocated; the question is whether it is bounded and well-understood.
+- **Recurring infrastructure cost** (moderate) — variable fees tied to connection or message volume. At 800 simultaneous browsers and average 2 edits per minute with opening-time bursts, volume costs on a per-connection or per-message model are material for a pilot budget.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers issue a GET request to a read endpoint every five seconds. The endpoint returns the current board state or a diff since a cursor revision the browser supplies. The server is stateless with respect to browsers; no persistent connection is maintained. Browsers naturally catch up after any gap by polling again with their last-known revision.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- Change freshness under load: **weak** — the polling interval adds 0–5 seconds of systematic lag to every committed edit, on top of commit processing time. During opening-time bursts, an editor's change may not reach peer browsers until the next poll cycle. The 15-second goal is met, but the mechanism uses most of the budget on idle intervals.
+- Read load under busy conditions: **weak** — 800 browsers polling every five seconds generates approximately 160 requests per second of baseline read traffic regardless of edit activity. This load is present even when the board is unchanged.
+- Operational independence: **strong** — no third-party dependency; stateless request handling.
+- Connection management complexity: **strong** — no persistent connections; no reconnect logic; browser state is a single cursor value.
+- Recurring infrastructure cost: **strong** — no variable vendor cost; read traffic is self-hosted.
+
+*Essential trade: simplest to operate, but adds systematic latency and sustained read load that scale with browser count rather than edit rate.*
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server maintains persistent WebSocket connections to browsers. When the server commits an edit, it pushes a change event immediately to all connected browsers. The change store maintains a numbered publication outbox; on reconnect, delivery replays events from the browser's last-received revision. The server owns connection state and must track active sessions; browsers implement reconnect logic and issue catch-up requests after a gap.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- Change freshness under load: **strong** — push-on-commit; browsers receive change events within milliseconds of the server writing the revision. No polling interval adds lag.
+- Read load under busy conditions: **strong** — the server pushes only when edits occur; no baseline polling traffic.
+- Operational independence: **strong** — no third-party dependency; the server owns connection state and the publication outbox.
+- Connection management complexity: **weak** — the server must hold state for up to 800 concurrent connections; delivery needs session tracking and outbox replay; browsers need reconnect logic and the ability to request missed events. These are bounded, well-understood WebSocket patterns, but they require explicit implementation in the delivery and browser-sync nodes.
+- Recurring infrastructure cost: **strong** — no variable vendor cost; connection state is self-hosted.
+
+*Essential trade: near-real-time delivery with no vendor dependency, at the cost of owning connection management and reconnect logic.*
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A third-party realtime service (such as Ably or Pusher) manages WebSocket connections on behalf of the server. The server publishes change events to the service via its API; browsers subscribe to the board's channel using the service's client SDK. The service handles connection management, reconnect, and event history. The server's delivery layer becomes a thin publisher rather than a session manager.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- Change freshness under load: **strong** — push-on-commit via the provider; delivery latency is the service's SLA.
+- Read load under busy conditions: **strong** — event-driven; no polling traffic.
+- Operational independence: **weak** — board availability is bounded by the provider's SLA; the browser-sync implementation couples to the provider's subscription model and SDK; migrating away requires rewriting the delivery and browser-sync nodes. Per-connection and per-message pricing introduces ongoing fees that scale with usage.
+- Connection management complexity: **partial** — the server's publish path is simple; the provider absorbs connection management and reconnect. The browser uses the provider's SDK rather than implementing reconnect directly. The trade is complexity exchanged for coupling and fees rather than eliminated.
+- Recurring infrastructure cost: **weak** — per-connection and per-message fees are real at 800 simultaneous browsers and opening-time burst volumes; these fees persist for the lifetime of the deployment.
+
+*Essential trade: lowest connection management work, at the cost of provider coupling, availability dependency, and ongoing usage-based fees.*
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+On the two major criteria, it is the only option that scores strong on both. Five-second polling achieves operational independence but sacrifices freshness and generates read load that scales with browser count, not edit rate — at 800 browsers, that is 160 baseline requests per second with a systematic 0–5 second lag baked in. A hosted realtime service achieves freshness but scores weak on operational independence: the board's availability becomes bounded by a third-party SLA, migration requires rewriting two design nodes, and per-connection fees are ongoing. Self-hosted WebSockets achieves strong freshness and strong operational independence.
+
+The connection management complexity is the accepted cost. The delivery and browser-sync nodes take on explicit, bounded responsibilities — session tracking, outbox replay, reconnect, and catch-up — that are standard WebSocket patterns with known solutions. This work is sized into those nodes rather than treated as incidental.
+
+The decision is taken *with* the tenet *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted*: the latitude to use a hosted service exists, but it is not exercised here. The choice turns primarily on operational independence and cost: a pilot deployment should not carry recurring per-connection fees or an external availability dependency before the board has proved its value.
+
+**Downstream obligations:** the change store must assign monotone revision numbers to committed edits and maintain a publication outbox; delivery must manage WebSocket sessions and support replay from a given revision; browser sync must maintain the connection, track the last-received revision, reconnect on drop, and request missed events.
