 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed edits are pushed to browsers over a self-hosted WebSocket server. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it adds 0–5 seconds of lag on every change and generates repeated read load during busy opening-time bursts. A hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring per-connection fees and provider coupling. The connection management and reconnect work is accepted as the operational cost of owning the stack. See `docs/decisions/websocket-delivery.md`.
